#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199266} :activity/id #uuid "64d036ec-bd13-5db0-909a-44c4f2ba1ce6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198360 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198234 :question/id #uuid "d154ea1a-bb3c-510d-abe3-dc1cf51558b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433504}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "2f924b6e-2f47-553e-b2e6-6d98b8c55641" :step/math-academy-id 26205 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199450 :tutorial/content "Consider a transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n![](images/fe/fefb7b82a6da1421bb96798fe6377e66199b50b8630dc396518927a0f3fe204b.svg)\n\nThe points in the $(u,v)$ plane where $\\mathbf T$ is *locally* non-invertible are known as **critical points**. \n\nWe've already seen that $\\mathbf T$ is locally non-invertible when the Jacobian determinant is zero. Therefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ are the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nLet's compute the critical points of the following transformation:\n\n![](images/8e/8e55b2da9a0c1fcf1ab901d361c50b652c906b92f0fcd4b158793f7782cfe5f4.svg)\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =4u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4u & -6v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4u\\cdot 1-(-6v)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when the Jacobian equals zero. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u-3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ consist of those points that lie on the line $2u-3v=0$  in the $uv$-plane, shown below. \n\n![](images/5c/5c544e3d3ecbdeaf7dc372b40ee0f37cb14e3ba292b8180a263c91103a9efe61.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f47e688-774b-5c89-a90d-ead52a871598" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4312 :tutorial/title "Critical Points of Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "16b2c818-03e6-5801-97b2-0ce8d225c3d5" :step/math-academy-id 26206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199460 :tutorial/content "Let's now state the so-called **inverse function theorem** for single-variable functions:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the graph below:\n\n![](images/4d/4dfd8b142b594a2ca4f0e39f9b8ca1e04018bf0efd63f9dc2235bd61e9f9d70e.png)\n\nIn this example, the function $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ and has a nonzero derivative. As a result, $f^{-1}$ exists in a neighborhood of $x=b$ and is continuously differentiable at this point.\n\nOn the other hand, if $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ but $f'(a) = 0,$ then one of two things can happen:\n\n- **Case 1**: The function $f$ does not have a local inverse at $a.$\n\n![](images/04/04ee310e6068e517aec42a67fa83837986f8807e1250b89b52e7a62e3975bb38.png)\n\nIn the example above, $f$ is not one-to-one in a neighborhood of $a,$ so it's not invertible there.\n\n- **Case 2**: The function $f$ has a local inverse at $a,$ but its inverse is not continuously differentiable at $b.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e2738a16f999d503fb295f4fd6535a854884468a4d190ffa537a4ec9295490c.png)\n\nIn the example above, $f$ is invertible in a neighborhood of $a,$ but $f^{-1}$ is not differentiable at $x=b$ since it has a vertical tangent at this point." :tutorial/id #uuid "c45037f7-31c7-5911-a00d-edaa4c5b86e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7036 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54cb06ef-1ffc-5b15-9147-a1baf70b985c" :step/math-academy-id 42404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198370 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198012 :question/id #uuid "f794d50a-cb44-5a6f-a922-8438a6192d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-11157"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527136}] :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "1b07afe2-9993-5d3c-bfe7-b98917224b3b" :step/math-academy-id 42423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199477 :tutorial/content "The inverse function theorem can be generalized to transformations too. We state it below:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf T:U \\to \\Bbb R^2$ be a $C^1$ (continuously differentiable) transformation on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ where *\n![](images/82/829c083606df36014d5b5b078b80c1c0ff1edb72f981880b4a1a6a16affb32dc.svg)\n\n*Further, suppose that $(u_0,v_0)$ is **not** a critical point of $\\mathbf T.$* Then,\n\n- *$\\mathbf T$ is locally invertible at $(u_0,v_0),$*\n\n- *$\\mathbf T^{-1},$ the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ is a $C^1$ function in a neighborhood of $(x_0,y_0),$ and*\n\n- *the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} ,$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04daa2a7-b824-543f-b8d6-79a5b54e4799" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7678 :tutorial/title "The Inverse Function Theorem for Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "63b8e23b-b4a6-5984-b37f-dab9c1ced3af" :step/math-academy-id 44589 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198042 :question/id #uuid "217fe5bd-4ea4-57b9-8772-bb6e517207bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12416"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049731}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479856}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "809a242a-d30e-5e4d-9b3e-fc6725358aac" :step/math-academy-id 44598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199249}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198489}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407322} :activity/id #uuid "7eb720ac-5358-5a94-bfe5-d2ea9292b4f9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879a74f3-cdb2-5fc9-83e0-82f1953090a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401188} {:answer/id #uuid "b598f6a3-a6bb-5202-8ed8-ac9c42eb7279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401189} {:answer/id #uuid "bca486aa-25ef-5c7c-9609-2ed1a4c5553a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401190} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3464e3-f53d-53d8-877c-0141084f3eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401191} {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192} :answer-field/id #uuid "753118cd-0a71-5bb3-aaee-283497ceb3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f05de000-4a38-5b78-b8b6-ab15cb5a35af" :question/math-academy-id "q-160473" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "1d0addec-158f-59f3-a8b0-a1fbeca5ea06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49fc877d-2b64-58a0-a27d-95f261c0a7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276131} {:answer/id #uuid "4db3508a-8d7f-5449-9c40-7069ccf6fd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm 2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276162} {:answer/id #uuid "855bca3a-ff78-5604-b854-6c63d00bbe2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=\\pm v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276671} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7df95b-b32d-5ca1-b866-576a47074953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277286} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeaac23-e098-5ed2-b6ba-88b5f1f7e94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277286} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc5c8f10-5fa6-51ed-b472-61ec07b1ffb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb96d7dc-7f59-56fa-90d1-c2a71b2fab70" :question/math-academy-id "q-160460" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "85b72007-63eb-5d72-bd12-04d202f52103" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4969b933-9252-5d6c-99a3-2d0800a47ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276127} {:answer/id #uuid "770127d5-5c20-5966-b228-70cc5f941617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276551} {:answer/id #uuid "b84e8d31-b0f2-5bc7-b250-aae11f693050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277111} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cce8a1-986b-5e0d-9d7a-5c50318cedd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277250} {:answer/id #uuid "d9290ebb-9e9d-5d8a-8ec2-a3ee34d8fd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277385}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277111} :answer-field/id #uuid "94528d39-40c7-5d22-bbd3-10b2d8db0c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1077730f-94dd-5875-b9c2-642bfe60bb08" :question/math-academy-id "q-160582" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y{e}^{xy} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{e}^{xy}}{x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9c3612bc-50a7-5cec-b6e2-2f8735720eb8"}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407320}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433508} :activity/id #uuid "9d89a91b-0f23-5034-8485-cf2fdb766717" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9ce46f-74e5-51fc-85f1-be6a4dc35d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433498} {:answer/id #uuid "144c14ba-18af-5f87-a114-1f0e70bc5c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(-1,-2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433499} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1b9805-9295-5f1f-93ad-1b87d622dbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433500} {:answer/id #uuid "2794a3de-45e3-5d1c-aaa5-e06f453a2bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433501} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eec4e3-df6c-5bff-a85a-74dee074332e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433502} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f46da7-c0a4-56d1-b636-f9591428aeed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ffd9297-ce86-5e32-becf-cbd84b607c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-160476" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}(u-1)^{3}-(v-2)^{3} \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=(u-1)^{3}-(v-2)^{3}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u-1)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-3(v-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3(u-1)^{2} & -3(v-2)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(u-1)^{2}\\cdot 1-(-3(v-2)^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2}=0$ defines the point $(1,2)$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "2829a17c-8a17-5054-a69a-9c845f86a147"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121cd8c0-d560-5b26-a5e1-a9a682d56bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348603} {:answer/id #uuid "704f578b-3d6d-5087-b641-678331beb665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349482} {:answer/id #uuid "765f759f-cb7f-500e-a2e6-effae823f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349533} {:answer/id #uuid "a195f1c2-37e2-5dd7-8c22-1fa039d5302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} {:answer/id #uuid "b0121869-6909-521a-9b62-122d348331d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbe94b8-88e8-57a5-bae1-d604a1f7732b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7079635f-c699-5683-9c56-da86db0d6f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-81319" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "552040e6-e34a-5420-b715-153752988504" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272de190-0e96-5d51-95d4-ff366ae72e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348785} {:answer/id #uuid "62b96416-b661-53cd-8fd9-4a67cc99ae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} {:answer/id #uuid "93ea44ae-a889-5898-8990-fdfc9599ae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349770} {:answer/id #uuid "cefdca57-4e7f-505d-ba98-23b6018b411a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350265} {:answer/id #uuid "f22a24d4-0975-5c9c-9994-b66abef9c47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} :answer-field/id #uuid "6831b19c-e658-54e3-b4bb-f0560cf60cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "24c29280-75c6-50b5-ad20-b15d70578fce" :question/math-academy-id "q-81305" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a48caee-df37-515d-8b93-7198deb33b28" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d467be4-da9c-5949-964e-fc1ece22124b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u+v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402618} {:answer/id #uuid "99d8765a-e6b9-5b6f-b95c-7ee236d3b208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u-v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402619} {:answer/id #uuid "a23b6f7f-749d-5d1a-b619-0c32cdbf023d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402620} {:answer/id #uuid "128c4bf5-7f24-5412-a170-6a74863be1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402621} {:answer/id #uuid "72a4bc7a-67e1-58b0-9c23-038668c40386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402620} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05d1ca1-306f-5d0c-970a-e108a73f246e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48feab89-768b-5b8e-8c0a-02f86fdff070" :question/math-academy-id "q-38245" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -8uv & =0 \\\\ uv & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $uv=0$ defines the pair of intersecting lines $u=0$ and $v=0$ in the $uv$-plane. Notice that these lines correspond to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "df267d9e-6bbe-5490-b116-b3284eaeaf33" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dd60f7-0060-5908-af95-4c108c3b1718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404165} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2acdfc-59b5-5eb0-aa0e-5c6158877fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5de86-0281-5ef0-9bac-c48c24b2a6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404167} {:answer/id #uuid "90292b65-61da-58e0-bd75-eda1b58d756e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404168} {:answer/id #uuid "22f6e316-1aa7-5c12-abf8-9b28b8e93f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} :answer-field/id #uuid "e87074f0-9d22-553d-826e-78e5774760d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59e9bc67-c967-5c58-a234-d26582651fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81279" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+3{v}^{2} \\\\ u-2v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+3{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u-2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 6v \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2)-6v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u+3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u+3v=0$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "e590a33c-a8f9-5732-980b-cf8843af4f45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433507}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433497}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486480} :activity/id #uuid "9906dd6e-f368-5644-ad97-3ccd3454dbd9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486477 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f3745d-f09c-5360-a931-21a667dd1d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465453} {:answer/id #uuid "21ed980e-4000-5687-a17b-d84e6f1e4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465454} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8bbd34-591c-5d0f-b655-42fc58838887" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "835fd9d2-ac5f-5fc7-a341-f53a230e3899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465455} {:answer/id #uuid "1205f76c-8469-5ede-a981-4f167eb37530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465455} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92c5843-42f4-52a3-a501-b15764eb3a62" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36cd71c2-430d-5fd7-a358-b102308c8d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465457} {:answer/id #uuid "71481a29-9e86-5894-95fe-7fecf6d21f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465457} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98b628c-ca32-5dd8-8891-b556d9bd4149" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4eb620b0-5fb5-5c19-a1ea-9934c615ac57" :question/math-academy-id "q-161857" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4b56f991-f12d-5895-8d7a-a6f922386e67"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ef2d77-bba0-583d-ab24-6864e8cb1120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321553} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6dc061-def5-52ec-97ef-1ebc1a91b58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321608} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dcda6c-6b89-5eeb-91a4-9597bb3375ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322563} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d659d-8fcd-5f7f-88f9-41d43214f667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322957} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c9d56d-f9b3-57f8-98d4-395a8bb6a219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322563} :answer-field/id #uuid "bae91dc2-4344-5852-b87e-1866ee0bd08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ba03a11-dea1-56d5-9626-63bbe9ed1384" :question/math-academy-id "q-38079" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-2v\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}={u}^{2}$ defines a pair of intersecting lines $v=u$ and $v=-u$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "4da312bf-59b4-5062-8ec0-dbcdcb029a0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121cd8c0-d560-5b26-a5e1-a9a682d56bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348603} {:answer/id #uuid "704f578b-3d6d-5087-b641-678331beb665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349482} {:answer/id #uuid "765f759f-cb7f-500e-a2e6-effae823f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349533} {:answer/id #uuid "a195f1c2-37e2-5dd7-8c22-1fa039d5302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} {:answer/id #uuid "b0121869-6909-521a-9b62-122d348331d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbe94b8-88e8-57a5-bae1-d604a1f7732b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7079635f-c699-5683-9c56-da86db0d6f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-81319" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f3a2d34-0995-5368-905a-f5f4b05fda88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81738919-7aa7-51e9-a640-81f3dcaec4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383524} {:answer/id #uuid "46ebbeb2-cd8e-59fa-897a-755f09186557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=-2v$ and $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383525} {:answer/id #uuid "21112086-2ed7-5be4-ac2f-eff59a4b2ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the hyperbola ${v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383526} {:answer/id #uuid "78535bbb-7c2d-52da-8e93-a0da8a8b205c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383527} {:answer/id #uuid "960bc4eb-19d3-58a0-8107-c7c207282b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383524} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f817ac-86b2-5ffa-80ca-0153f1c9510b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f0fb416-ba54-5520-a3cd-23b891dc09c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-160477" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+2v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=u{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+2v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 2uv \\\\ 2u & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={v}^{2}\\cdot 2-2uv\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v & =0 \\\\ v(2v-4{u}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v(2v-4{u}^{2})=0$ defines the line $v=0,$ i.e., the $u$-axis, and the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "85fa7b96-b748-5bcb-8112-da4536f9e0d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e9a8a63-07dd-5f16-a2f8-3b7c74281739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485910} {:answer/id #uuid "6b09dc02-1831-530e-86c4-190d1b8a2dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485947} {:answer/id #uuid "7766cddb-20aa-5b50-8013-76522352d29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485971} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b8a16-21fd-5169-addf-da191e48ae8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486022} {:answer/id #uuid "9673978f-ee00-5dbf-beeb-1fda93353e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485910} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0df0bc-bf6e-51c0-bdb6-19b9ad7ef142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5787fa90-c666-5c53-9045-790fb21cdab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160577" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ 3y & 3x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 3x-(-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}})\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =\\frac{9x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8799c879-5fd1-5dbd-8c44-d6fe6d0c6a5b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486477}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485758}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502213} :activity/id #uuid "68961a22-241c-5590-ba4b-ccd92cb14402" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502184 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5476ef-9967-5eb9-8009-75dc10f39af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349022} {:answer/id #uuid "4a194f65-cf15-535b-ab7d-1b24dafeab09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349121} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a0b613-aa43-5173-a700-526e75ebfed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349884} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2f2e7e-62f9-51c6-9d9c-990e6ed9e67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350380} {:answer/id #uuid "e587527b-1eec-5e49-adc0-ab90da7d05db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349884} :answer-field/id #uuid "7895d7a9-cd67-552f-bbe6-5fc99e21c22e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c73b659-30f6-5b1c-afe8-2ef5c03763e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81333" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y \\\\ x+2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x-y$ and $v(x,y)=x+2y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "66c204cb-d446-5e69-ba93-b51435fae35f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502195}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49fc877d-2b64-58a0-a27d-95f261c0a7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276131} {:answer/id #uuid "4db3508a-8d7f-5449-9c40-7069ccf6fd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm 2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276162} {:answer/id #uuid "855bca3a-ff78-5604-b854-6c63d00bbe2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=\\pm v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276671} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7df95b-b32d-5ca1-b866-576a47074953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277286} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeaac23-e098-5ed2-b6ba-88b5f1f7e94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277286} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc5c8f10-5fa6-51ed-b472-61ec07b1ffb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb96d7dc-7f59-56fa-90d1-c2a71b2fab70" :question/math-academy-id "q-160460" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "a476947a-9128-5e05-b499-9b539dccc98c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9ce46f-74e5-51fc-85f1-be6a4dc35d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433498} {:answer/id #uuid "144c14ba-18af-5f87-a114-1f0e70bc5c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(-1,-2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433499} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1b9805-9295-5f1f-93ad-1b87d622dbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433500} {:answer/id #uuid "2794a3de-45e3-5d1c-aaa5-e06f453a2bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433501} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eec4e3-df6c-5bff-a85a-74dee074332e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433502} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f46da7-c0a4-56d1-b636-f9591428aeed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ffd9297-ce86-5e32-becf-cbd84b607c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-160476" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}(u-1)^{3}-(v-2)^{3} \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=(u-1)^{3}-(v-2)^{3}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u-1)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-3(v-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3(u-1)^{2} & -3(v-2)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(u-1)^{2}\\cdot 1-(-3(v-2)^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2}=0$ defines the point $(1,2)$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "dff77846-631b-5b22-8d07-2a4fe05cca8d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502212}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502212 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8316c3-945b-5d09-a275-e0c583f57a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275895} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b7c50f-b475-5cd3-a478-644d87193dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277324} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb6f0a5-bfd6-5a0f-aea6-85ae01e06079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277401} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fd723e-649c-5294-ae81-2a842e9e6b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277455} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ad5de4-55a8-578b-8fc5-a8a2b67c0b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277465} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c2b48aa-f3d5-54e7-9a9a-986efee1cf0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1615e71e-cacf-55f4-8706-f141063daf67" :question/math-academy-id "q-160479" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x-2y \\\\ 3x+4y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=x-2y$ and $v(x,y)=3x+4y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fb99b038-1031-5a63-aed5-c29598b71cc2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502213 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a499ca4-07df-5c8d-b83c-a9910cc2fe6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=v$ and $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404158} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf255f1-3d51-5826-98c1-93901517d605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404159} {:answer/id #uuid "5da5054c-581f-5f31-9886-dad4f588043d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404160} {:answer/id #uuid "6060e83f-92e8-5d62-986a-7a4aa49b2792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404161} {:answer/id #uuid "1669bc78-1efd-58f9-91e9-a08a66025cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0$ and on the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404162} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd706ed-a6fa-5777-8fba-b9aa9ded06db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "82fb995e-103e-58b4-a8e9-4c6b2963c1a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81273" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}\\,\\,v \\\\ u+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}\\,\\,v$ and $y(u,v)=u+{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={u}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2uv & {u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2uv\\cdot 2v-{u}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4u{v}^{2}-{u}^{2} \\\\  & =u(4{v}^{2}-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ u(4{v}^{2}-u) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equation $u(4{v}^{2}-u)=0$ defines a pair of intersecting curves in the $uv$-plane: the line $u=0$ and the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "fd04f09e-0dfd-514c-b0d1-078c32dd4101" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502184}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501453}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527283} :activity/id #uuid "d4d7c1e7-1e75-5e96-a1f2-125d61b017ca" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7b11e3-ec58-5e4e-af9c-4b27fe57afa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275572} {:answer/id #uuid "1b48c506-a496-56c6-9f38-211be2533d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275730} {:answer/id #uuid "877b2e30-bb6d-567a-a22b-1e9d08ebe6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276699} {:answer/id #uuid "c92b975d-bcee-5cb4-8f7c-c5b62dcd4759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277263} {:answer/id #uuid "d27bbf8b-f7d8-58e5-a38d-897bd7f54bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277335} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b872a64-713f-5527-a8d1-f8a1304b926e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed8207bc-4d78-5a97-b503-5177675ffb34" :question/math-academy-id "q-160581" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-x+7y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=-x+7y$ and $v(x,y)=3x-y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3-7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "139d7e34-eb4e-5c83-930d-e178bce0f1f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da512ca-3f7f-55fa-ba92-0e0c6f6c21be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526563} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9f7694-d0a1-5a9c-8f7b-8ada526129f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{3v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526627} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e1f31b-7a73-5f60-aa37-ebb1960532b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526815} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ff2ce6-b380-5ab9-9fa2-41399e5c2bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526881} {:answer/id #uuid "cabf98b2-4e8b-519a-86a9-7f344c20fcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526881} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa3cd04-7b64-5645-9bfe-bb149f8d1b73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "216ab53f-9c7f-5387-ad09-67452d907209" :question/math-academy-id "q-160572" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x}{y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ \\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y\\cdot (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})-x\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "51036647-b610-5b0a-9fd7-d9d58d8980da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527234}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dd60f7-0060-5908-af95-4c108c3b1718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404165} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2acdfc-59b5-5eb0-aa0e-5c6158877fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5de86-0281-5ef0-9bac-c48c24b2a6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404167} {:answer/id #uuid "90292b65-61da-58e0-bd75-eda1b58d756e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404168} {:answer/id #uuid "22f6e316-1aa7-5c12-abf8-9b28b8e93f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} :answer-field/id #uuid "e87074f0-9d22-553d-826e-78e5774760d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59e9bc67-c967-5c58-a234-d26582651fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81279" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+3{v}^{2} \\\\ u-2v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+3{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u-2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 6v \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2)-6v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u+3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u+3v=0$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "631c1ffc-cc2d-54ee-9534-d0fd945d1a0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2141fe03-5609-506c-ae00-264723dbff1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275778} {:answer/id #uuid "60c950bb-e6b5-566d-a83e-c5ddb2dd1bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276346} {:answer/id #uuid "90a0356c-7c2b-5475-809e-f584e2ea2afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276788} {:answer/id #uuid "9e509876-60c7-502b-a36b-eb375b31d6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276900} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e144d9-a262-5093-84e5-e89abb2f6f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277192} :answer-field/id #uuid "e88fdffa-ff8d-5cae-a661-8b8827ff078f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bcef19dc-e0d9-5a18-86b4-519865d6c64e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160599" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \nHere, we have $u(x,y)=xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{y} \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96a24d2f-6bfd-5bae-b385-4ad5b9f4b243"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9da3a0-9b03-595c-bf6b-d6f24859c749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0,$ and $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348538} {:answer/id #uuid "6239141a-53ca-56ad-bb72-682061cb903b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0,$ $u=1,$ and $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349344} {:answer/id #uuid "a123a690-23eb-5260-8eb8-ee8dcc2d1499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0,$ and $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349876} {:answer/id #uuid "c479b1d9-8b7a-5b9b-a1c1-da3550cc5252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=v,$ and $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350182} {:answer/id #uuid "f98c9804-a52a-5997-9ebe-f863a7748737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=v$ and $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349344} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1bee6f-9d9a-5e1c-a014-769f23b83e3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ccef9016-7753-56bc-8e3b-a3164aadcab2" :question/math-academy-id "q-81275" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v(u-1)^{2} \\\\ v{u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=v(u-1)^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=v{u}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2(u-1)v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =(u-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2(u-1)v & (u-1)^{2} \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(u-1)v\\cdot {u}^{2}-(u-1)^{2}\\cdot 2uv \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}(u-1)v-2u(u-1)^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}(u-1)v-2u(u-1)^{2}v & =0 \\\\ 2u(u-1)v(u-(u-1)) & =0 \\\\ 2u(u-1)v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u(u-1)v=0$ defines the lines $u=0,$ $u=1,$ and $v=0$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "e82f2471-fe74-5e55-b55f-d5691e572e48" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527257}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526494}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129892} :activity/id #uuid "87e23de1-746c-5124-9751-bee5befcb28f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048554 :topic/id #uuid "a93bb5a8-5344-5f11-a0fd-45410668ecd6" :topic/math-academy-id 1984} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130327 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to compute the tangent plane to the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k,$ where $k$ is a constant, at some point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0).$ \n\nAs an example, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2 +y^2+z.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that we wish to find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=  (1,1, 2).$ \n\nEvaluating $f$ at the point $(1,1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = (1)^2 +(1)^2+2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of our level surface is\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at $(1,1,2),$ we can use the following result:\n\n  *Given a hypersurface $w = f(x,y,z)$ and a point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0,y_0,z_0)$ that lies in the domain of $f$, the gradient $\\nabla \\! f (\\mathbf x_0)$ is normal to the tangent plane of the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k$ at the point $\\mathbf x_0$*. \n\n![](images/11/1140e80fbb2efd684311cb7625e5b0be361d6fb951036253f57b9e51234de3ee.png)\n\n       \n\nWe can now find the equation of the tangent plane to our level surface.\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf x_0 = (1,1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2) & =2(1)\\text{ }i+2(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+2\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,  we write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\nabla f(1,1,2) \\cdot \\langle x-1, \\: y-1, \\: z-2 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\langle 2,\\:2,\\:1\\rangle\\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1, z-2\\rangle &= 0\\[3pt]\n      2(x-1)+ 2(y-1) + (z-2) & =0\\\n      2x +2y +z -6&=0   \n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f7db883-9d6e-5a67-97d4-20274467a1e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2500 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "13f10d0e-ddd7-5833-b73f-b3f00ff7016a" :step/math-academy-id 11201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129893}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129079 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128893 :question/id #uuid "09499f38-a497-5f59-9cb2-0882a5853320" :question/math-academy-id "e-3368"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da47f01b-0db0-586f-9d55-5bd4c952afb6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493943}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent to Plane a Level Surface"} :step/id #uuid "6d9d3ca6-ae9b-5ac5-a35f-80fa6628ef2f" :step/math-academy-id 11204 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128998 :question/id #uuid "9091e2dc-5b05-5ae7-a498-9de0483ec375" :question/math-academy-id "e-6183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475934}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Plane of a Surface Given Implicitly Using the Gradient Vector"} :step/id #uuid "f4dac68d-5f9a-5fba-b2c5-3ea5a9ec6475" :step/math-academy-id 23124}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Level Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129332}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494072} :activity/id #uuid "63e75d2f-a87d-5228-8980-4d6f3fc9a47a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048554 :topic/id #uuid "a93bb5a8-5344-5f11-a0fd-45410668ecd6" :topic/math-academy-id 1984} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c75f8c9-6d90-5e2f-9fd0-6d4f8cbd173a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,e,2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+2,\\,z+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493465} {:answer/id #uuid "3050b9e4-da08-5715-ad5a-8caf10a32624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-e,-2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493472} {:answer/id #uuid "472c72f7-428a-5c92-9b64-36649f199a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨e,1,2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493511} {:answer/id #uuid "67d37bdf-2be9-5b4b-b6fd-993f6357958d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,e,-2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493545} {:answer/id #uuid "a09c4894-0619-505a-853a-743d133d9d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,e,-e⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+2,\\,z+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493472} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ccc9b7-9d2d-5fab-9305-0a2ddb6c4989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1c45efd-b454-595e-a933-aae318d15f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-102700" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-{e}^{z}\\text{ }j-y{e}^{z}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2,1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\text{ }i-{e}^{1}\\text{ }j-(2){e}^{1}k \\\\  & =2\\,i-e\\,j-2ek \\\\  & =⟨2,-e,-2e⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-e,-2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7ecf0ade-c143-56c2-83e7-b60005d8d75d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8438dc-a715-5c0c-aab2-6e47f02ae119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x-y+2z-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236008} {:answer/id #uuid "85b07a3f-7374-5a9c-b7c1-947842c86d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+3y+z+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236650} {:answer/id #uuid "94a5b114-54d1-55a1-b257-cc17cc5b9faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y+5z-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236759} {:answer/id #uuid "b02e51c4-263c-5a4d-80f9-9315bea2d383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+4y-3z+15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236937} {:answer/id #uuid "c93bc415-579e-5faf-ba97-c8c120703f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+4y+5z-18=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237093} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e7ba58-6e3b-5d46-8ec2-848013d679c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a47eb1cf-14ab-540e-8110-7aec36247dcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-102711" :question/problem "Consider the surface $xy{z}^{2}=6-z.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(1,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{z}^{2} & =6-z \\\\ xy{z}^{2}+z & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+(x{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2xyz+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1,2) & =(1)(2)^{2}\\text{ }i+(1)(2)^{2}\\text{ }j+(2(1)(1)(2)+1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i+4j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨4,4,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,4,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-1)+4(y-1)+5(z-2) & =0 \\\\ 4x+4y+5z-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8542cb45-fe3e-585c-beae-8f3803d3fd88"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193c9497-7ea6-5a1d-b9f5-a009ce93ca23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-y+12z-24=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366817} {:answer/id #uuid "f29eacee-8051-57cb-aee4-51b137c18ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-y+z+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366818} {:answer/id #uuid "710265a2-6141-5b27-8548-bda3797d8905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-2y-5z+11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366819} {:answer/id #uuid "d24d01c2-6eb5-5662-b203-5deae9c0dd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-3y-4z+24=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366820} {:answer/id #uuid "8c31a377-21c2-5c10-92eb-4e6439b5d5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y-4z+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366817} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac4c269d-3ccc-505a-8b3b-1f9e614487fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "baefe5d5-6705-561e-af37-627a61278732" :question/math-academy-id "q-102704" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=6{x}^{2}-y+{z}^{3}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes  through the point where $(x,y,z)=(\\frac{1}{2},3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(\\frac{1}{2},3,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}-y+{z}^{3})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}-y+{z}^{3})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6{x}^{2}-y+{z}^{3})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =12x\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j+3{z}^{2}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(\\frac{1}{2},3,2) & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j+3(2)^{2}k \\\\  & =6\\,i-\\,j+12k \\\\  & =⟨6,-1,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},3,2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y-3,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨6,-1,12⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y-3,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6(x-\\frac{1}{2})-(y-3)+12(z-2) & =0 \\\\ 6x-y+12z-24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "867c6d88-e80e-5619-af3d-96e820e79d18" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494027}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cd2801-6575-503c-8973-e5d77533e487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y+2z-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321465} {:answer/id #uuid "740d2fc5-1db2-531b-9591-8ebbee71f486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-4y+z+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322054} {:answer/id #uuid "adb3e2fe-6b96-5d2f-a240-7193b0f2a939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322509} {:answer/id #uuid "c2da99c8-1b83-5384-b154-4692549ce45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-2z-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322659} {:answer/id #uuid "c69134c6-e59d-5120-8af3-0b8bb7f8b190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y+2z+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322687} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e18b7a8-1caf-5bbf-be09-d1d09a6d4c0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c6c7087-87b2-59e1-b5dd-8db545cc6eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36765" :question/problem "Consider the surface ${x}^{2}=-xyz.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(4,2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =-xyz \\\\ {x}^{2}+xyz & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(4,2,-2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xyz)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xyz)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(4,2,-2) & =(2(4)+2(-2))\\text{ }i+4(-2)\\text{ }j+4(2)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i-8j+8\\,k \\\\  & =⟨4,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(4,2,-2)\\cdot ⟨x-4,\\,y-2,\\,z+2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,-8,8⟩\\cdot ⟨x-4,\\,y-2,\\,z+2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-4)+(-8)(y-2)+8(z+2) & =0 \\\\ 4x-8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ x-2y+2z+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b8b241c5-9ed0-5065-b6d8-8a3dd561fd54" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494023}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a034a0-40db-53e2-8743-0500f64927fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,1,-e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-e,\\,y-e,\\,z-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493426} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8e49ba-c572-585b-9000-22f731c9e31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨e,e,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493516} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd70068-b6fc-58b5-a8fd-0b6d0f6cdc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,1,-e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493590} {:answer/id #uuid "a4259989-16f4-5162-93ea-522cff1adbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,1,e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493652} {:answer/id #uuid "b48376c2-fa7d-5ea7-a3f5-9c2f64a3454e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨e,e,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1,\\,z+e⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493516} :answer-field/id #uuid "675858d2-09f1-5094-a85f-2db7923cff6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39528896-10e8-5126-bdec-f26e9bdcc487" :question/math-academy-id "q-36334" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}-z$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j-k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1,e) & ={e}^{1}\\text{ }i+(1){e}^{1}\\text{ }j-k \\\\  & =e\\,i+e\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨e,e,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨e,e,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f7e115a4-0109-5dcd-be67-0eff6d8c34d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494051}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Level Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493376}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125361} :activity/id #uuid "e6e3df9f-205d-5b79-a182-6a881367dca2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/id #uuid "bd333eb5-72e6-5eaa-be12-c6bf36306b90" :topic/math-academy-id 1910} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125768 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n![](images/d9/d9388f46f84cc0c6b1d7e5cf43ee973085366ae4f44cfe74515c59bfc1994662.svg)\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and ![](images/f9/f99f37621c20057077eb7c6e23690a45159cfe1b665c6b88b4a604f8687728bf.svg) Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overset{{P}_{B\\to C}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{[v]_{B}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "827e9581-9fde-5941-ad91-2c364796e5b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "910841a1-0516-546a-8aac-f188c965b763" :step/math-academy-id 17537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124308 :question/id #uuid "922b0f79-2c87-56f9-bf8a-18711a2a09d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440466}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "ad493885-054b-5d5e-a186-80a2c3801b8b" :step/math-academy-id 17538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124309 :question/id #uuid "a3911ca2-a357-5f37-8abc-e01e5518f24b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4840"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486444}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "45243fec-9ed2-54d8-b6ef-0d2c0a1e7087" :step/math-academy-id 17541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124310 :question/id #uuid "a2d3629f-5b7c-5b65-998f-8754ff11e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4841"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486397}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "9fa2d20c-055f-5364-a1f7-12e38362db0d" :step/math-academy-id 17542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124384 :question/id #uuid "f04f55d3-40ec-52a8-a762-d8ac7090b8b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048920}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440468}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"} :step/id #uuid "2c41659c-3fb0-58d9-904f-2296e677eb6c" :step/math-academy-id 26946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125822 :tutorial/content "We've seen that to find the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to a basis $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn expanded form, the formula looks as follows:\n![](images/89/890f34433211786e2cb8aa71057de40bdf3b12678c2bbb5a79ef5e680555937f.svg)\n\nAlso, remember that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ and from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:\n\n$$\nP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\n\t\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfffdf8e-9e9f-5304-8249-54a094cc7c11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4435 :tutorial/title "The Change-of-Basis Formula in Expanded Form"} :step/id #uuid "4c4259ae-ebd7-5d31-ae41-72156cefc066" :step/math-academy-id 27272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125708 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Now, suppose we would like to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ \n\n- We first need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+x_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_1,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/58/58010a2a5206e3fc27e3d924de70cf32dcc92c3578bdb2821ace7278fbb223cf.svg)\n\n- Similarly, we need to find $y_1$ and $y_2$ such that \n\n$$\ny_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+y_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/68/68788aa3e388c5cba14662a947c1b7afaacb95f611c256d11a7a6d7614c352b9.svg)\n\nWe can solve both systems using Gaussian elimination. However, we can do that simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/4b/4b00a4cbd7831f6da6acd06a087d5453bd455a89eb81f6b3036b1b572bb064d1.svg)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get a matrix of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {x}_{1} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}_{2} & {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the required coefficients will be stored in the columns on the right-hand side." :tutorial/id #uuid "d42ee304-2e1f-5cc2-b334-65d083ea7323" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13287 :tutorial/title "Using Gaussian Elimination to Find the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis"} :step/id #uuid "184b8c31-de1a-5e34-ad87-2f626c830182" :step/math-academy-id 462199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125498}}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124710}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436253} :activity/id #uuid "3b5b7055-d6b7-5899-a741-daa60aef491a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/id #uuid "bd333eb5-72e6-5eaa-be12-c6bf36306b90" :topic/math-academy-id 1910} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef569771-c4e7-5291-a02a-eccb98516f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 42\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386698} {:answer/id #uuid "19f40148-c027-5e57-83ca-f9ce5e2e487f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386699} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dbac90-d79d-5e74-a3e8-3e28846fd99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386700} {:answer/id #uuid "8aadec7c-66e6-5275-836d-0b2d825fbd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386701} {:answer/id #uuid "54154bb7-e3f6-55f0-b690-aed92908791b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386701} :answer-field/id #uuid "30675a92-d5d0-5d0c-a77d-9c525c8b8db4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ed39867-0f3e-5f3d-ab65-536828c9ba67" :question/math-academy-id "q-113573" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-8)-(-3)\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4de2c56b-39c0-5e14-a7fb-e708db903e6f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "426822c2-147e-581d-8735-96a3bffe8321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436236} {:answer/id #uuid "5c39bc95-ae02-5c17-9a57-15db094b94c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436238} {:answer/id #uuid "89f6cf40-819f-5c6b-aeaf-667b443a0b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436240} {:answer/id #uuid "e55ca324-fff5-5f74-ad7a-3c6af6f7f1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436244} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9b888d-cda8-5e1c-bef9-2a98c4f74288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436238} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1014636-1510-50f6-b488-e3fe8d24c13d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e4c9378-b054-5da6-b199-f46a138a46d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-113603" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4-6\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f40a8b0-4293-52f4-8c95-85c75998d506" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05662116-9ed5-51f3-a508-b1ed64f2e349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244359} {:answer/id #uuid "33a9596f-e4e0-5e92-b079-84b8cbdda157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244776} {:answer/id #uuid "7294001d-9a70-5e64-8833-bfc2b3f3c0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245337} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b79f00-6365-55a2-9673-e57f0f1960c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245947} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b08e24-4041-566d-9201-1f4cbd15de31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244359} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b31fac-75a2-50ec-9978-bbb512fe8ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "162a4f62-2177-5623-998d-5746dbddde07" :question/math-academy-id "q-113606" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77dfbfe9-ef7f-5713-be40-7bce1749b9a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e412cca-3de6-5980-bb99-cf3bccf43fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321257} {:answer/id #uuid "137c55e3-1ce7-52fa-871f-4778b481186a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321301} {:answer/id #uuid "930eac4b-99c1-548d-911a-4f0dbf0771a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322310} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd98043-a25b-5027-a9f0-593fb49217d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322391} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cab1f4-06ce-5d69-9f7d-a3f736d395e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322391} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e47d3d8-0d42-5d02-8f6a-ceceea2fa24c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "76994fe0-8052-50ba-9dfa-9fe567d9b172" :question/math-academy-id "q-36485" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2ad54ea-289c-5268-9e48-3009985055d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef4749a-0f85-5a2d-a5e3-df28976db7b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436237} {:answer/id #uuid "75556eec-854e-532d-9072-0581f7715993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436239} {:answer/id #uuid "a95465bd-ca1b-539b-b564-dc5450e2c673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436241} {:answer/id #uuid "d0811b3a-bd66-51ab-9df6-b54f8983cb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436242} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c4b9b8-c843-5d6e-98b0-ea1b8a725222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436237} :answer-field/id #uuid "54566980-7132-5fea-9868-d89904943d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d0458bf-0ded-5bc5-82a5-015d07a4533d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113615" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f5e3b29d-0add-527d-a99b-25156b6317ea"}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436235}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486512} :activity/id #uuid "123b57a8-59a0-5d9a-805f-fed4b8283678" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/id #uuid "bd333eb5-72e6-5eaa-be12-c6bf36306b90" :topic/math-academy-id 1910} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e5944a2-6f72-5f21-8b24-e64767793229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486018} {:answer/id #uuid "9f66dc53-0b31-5237-969a-2397bba21b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486050} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2979c6-49e8-5225-824c-1a6f38548151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486078} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffe2c5-8479-52b3-b64d-d795dd8a896a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486084} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cc0c71-6572-5be7-b79e-f4264330ac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486018} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71b80ef-6d4c-597c-814a-17f8214907ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72bf6680-369b-5d8c-9e49-1546d6428898" :question/math-academy-id "q-113598" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "50c856b2-5de1-5fc6-b8b6-216f5affc71d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277d51c6-ba77-5bde-a9da-6f2d8333899a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348786} {:answer/id #uuid "36849b8b-2c62-5eb1-bb53-ebb31890e317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348938} {:answer/id #uuid "526976b5-686a-54a2-a200-041c5d48d668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349197} {:answer/id #uuid "8056b724-3039-56ac-ac48-651200dd450e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349623} {:answer/id #uuid "9abeb6bd-73c2-5d7d-9344-2f69c899f0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348938} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8cbbee3-2e8a-56b1-9089-55eda08012bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49aa77db-aeb3-5225-bb69-221318751e9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82718" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b02f895b-b4b9-567f-99da-8866008266c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b3fc777-c87e-5494-9361-753fc99b8a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417145} {:answer/id #uuid "af2e1656-5790-518b-ae95-17ddec42b6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417157} {:answer/id #uuid "c820d187-6507-5a27-8119-ca85d381bd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417165} {:answer/id #uuid "d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417166} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368894-a420-5e78-a8ce-445dfc038e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417166} :answer-field/id #uuid "77968fba-758e-5c47-b717-abe8d60fbc59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "618e1711-d591-5872-b815-9d14ad33b674" :question/math-academy-id "q-37160" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 16-3\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cea4b00d-cc11-568c-8bd2-36b2db4582ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1696c49c-b8ba-5c9f-a1e2-a234d7a12381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485804} {:answer/id #uuid "33eeaaae-80c9-51a7-944e-f618a820b3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485862} {:answer/id #uuid "35031deb-a4ba-5495-89d6-18fe7aca9f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485864} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2528e3-8cd1-5c53-9f19-daeb351a37d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486101} {:answer/id #uuid "e673df76-6d49-52f2-85e7-cffc12218f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486101} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0731c0a-4746-54da-b38a-46b9b2e964b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae7fdbe5-ac31-5cc5-8e79-33235643a822" :question/math-academy-id "q-36502" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 10-6\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7f877e0-9517-58da-83be-55a89211a009" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15690e8b-c556-578a-a848-9e2012319066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485801} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce04a03-a227-57a9-bb1f-9d7b6996fb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485848} {:answer/id #uuid "972d0aed-9872-5a09-9680-0c04b590dfee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486037} {:answer/id #uuid "b85661ff-f141-5e76-a9a5-e29f75d6b6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486097} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bcf6bc-21ea-5ab1-b19b-a0bda85a2f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486097} :answer-field/id #uuid "f917e741-700b-51ed-9e8c-db10a05c79b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd3d1f8f-1ec9-5277-bd94-fc774bb919de" :question/math-academy-id "q-113595" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7ff6d11-7ab8-5df7-ab96-cc2f9b5a933c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486482}}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485749}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125476} :activity/id #uuid "42877e38-df38-5cf7-8778-f8ef552c82f7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102 :topic/id #uuid "dab75efc-7738-59c2-a66f-ba93589a5db9" :topic/math-academy-id 1925} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124562 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124368 :question/id #uuid "2230e2dd-e47a-5db3-bb07-f89d0396440b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6573"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427171}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"} :step/id #uuid "69c76fa5-15ee-51c5-b1e2-89538f46319f" :step/math-academy-id 24205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124563 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124369 :question/id #uuid "bd03341b-1190-51fa-b3f0-6fd921e3430c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6574"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427172}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"} :step/id #uuid "64adf3c9-6fdf-55fa-9cb4-7355522c084d" :step/math-academy-id 24206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125476 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125812 :tutorial/content "If we know one solution to a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, we can use the **reduction of order method** to find a second solution to the equation. \n\nIt works like this: if we know that some function $y_1(t)$ is a solution, then we let $y_2(t) = u(t) y_1(t)$ for some unknown function $u(t),$ and we write down a differential equation in terms of $u.$ Solving this equation, and then computing $y_2,$ gives us our second solution.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given that $y_1(t) = \\sin{4t}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's use the reduction of order method to find a second solution $y_2(t).$\n\nAccording to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)\\sin{4t}$ for some unknown function $u(t).$ Then, we write the differential equation in terms of $u.$ To do that, we first differentiate with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u\\operatorname{sin}4t \\\\ {y}_{2}^{'} & ={u}^{'}\\operatorname{sin}4t+4u\\operatorname{cos}4t \\\\ {y}_{2}^{''} & ={u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t-16u\\operatorname{sin}4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t-16u\\operatorname{sin}4t)+16u\\operatorname{sin}4t & =0 \\\\ {u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t & =0 \\\\ {u}^{''} & =-8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cot}4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' = -8w\\cot{4t}.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 \\csc^2{4t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1 \\csc^2{4t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = -\\dfrac{c_1}{4}\\cot{4t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u \\sin{4t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = \\sin{4t}\\left( -\\dfrac{c_1}{4}\\cot{4t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = -4$ and $c_2 = 0,$ then we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = \\sin{4t}\\left( \\cot{4t} \\right) = \\cos{4t}.\n$$\n\nWhen selecting our constants $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we should try to get a solution that is \"different\" from the first. We will define precisely what we mean by \"different\" soon, but loosely speaking we should aim for a $y_2(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- None of the terms is a constant multiple of the first solution (here, we accomplished this by choosing $c_2=0$).\n\n- The remaining terms are as simple as possible (here, we accomplished this by choosing $c_1=-4$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "af7458ab-92f7-5672-99ff-ffe77fdeeed1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4086 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a6adb1a-163b-5824-9043-b2e7539b66f3" :step/math-academy-id 24522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125474}}] :activity/title "Reduction of Order" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124719}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427176} :activity/id #uuid "ec3589cd-b2eb-5a12-b12d-dfa519698fc4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102 :topic/id #uuid "dab75efc-7738-59c2-a66f-ba93589a5db9" :topic/math-academy-id 1925} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093ed2aa-2b8c-5168-874c-f820cb49ad56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333073} {:answer/id #uuid "5d674283-f300-5b61-b687-08e0a2a4f93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333843} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef5037c-c9e5-51c3-8f13-71949d2ee3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334263} {:answer/id #uuid "987f5542-c24a-502c-b507-09ab20af49fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334340} {:answer/id #uuid "e593b34e-ca61-5493-979a-cdf8d1326e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}+7{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334987} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2aa559f-27df-59e2-a494-d0880512671b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a18ec5a-2e1b-5a0c-b567-88ee298d1b67" :question/math-academy-id "q-64995" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}-5y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=ux.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=ux$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}x+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′})+5x({u}^{′}x+u)-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5ux-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+7{u}^{′}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+7{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+7{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "06844a65-63a3-5579-82b4-67520af49c4a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427175}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427174 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "526bd235-8c14-5294-9466-6a7e8c18b8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427148} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2a2093-3771-5e44-84d7-a86917edc153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427152} {:answer/id #uuid "bec5beb9-da1e-55e4-9083-b45422a210b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-3{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427154} {:answer/id #uuid "bf107e21-60dc-5cea-ad5c-d09d313718a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427155} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe49dcb-dfb5-57aa-ac26-2c33b1844fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427148} :answer-field/id #uuid "27822b6f-f9aa-52c3-b623-d6079dfb84b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3cbf3dd8-a285-5e19-9174-c286d8ed0845" :question/math-academy-id "q-64994" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-4u\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-4u\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+4u\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ee0e41e-0081-57b1-b63e-73976c761c93" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427175 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b214ce-ab5e-5f5c-a969-dbab5f241399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}+4{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427146} {:answer/id #uuid "935207a8-801a-5dc5-9425-a3cd78674d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427150} {:answer/id #uuid "97c83eab-28ad-55eb-91f3-21e77e24722a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-2x{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427151} {:answer/id #uuid "c6393b4b-5ac6-54cd-828e-f2c5d96b68e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427156} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3901fc-4414-5f50-8377-cc29adff3e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+4x{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427150} :answer-field/id #uuid "47dbd607-90bc-550b-8e0e-ad2915edaeb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "879a358b-954a-58b1-b6bd-64affbba8f30" :question/math-academy-id "q-102795" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-1}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{x}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{-1}-u{x}^{-2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{-2}+2u{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{-2}+2u{x}^{-3})+4x({u}^{′}{x}^{-1}-u{x}^{-2})+2u{x}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x-2{u}^{′}+2u{x}^{-1}+4{u}^{′}-4u{x}^{-1}+2u{x}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "90e390d3-2285-5647-8dee-72ff1477f1a3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427177}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427176 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5624fee0-ac6c-5a27-a00d-a533ce8462a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427149} {:answer/id #uuid "c66f0cef-0e9d-5e23-a546-875b0af82f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427157} {:answer/id #uuid "cb87e4e8-0d55-5f56-909c-77cfc9ff1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427159} {:answer/id #uuid "e92d5e88-cebd-5955-be19-3ce4ea54b887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427161} {:answer/id #uuid "fc51c4e7-1541-5022-aa97-d43e54b6050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427149} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d2223c-c444-5eb2-b0e0-62ae52349f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0006acdb-45b3-5900-8e6e-eb4bda5a2c89" :question/math-academy-id "q-64893" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}={e}^{x}({u}^{′}+u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u)-{e}^{x}(u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u-u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+2w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-2x}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}{e}^{-2x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}(-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}{e}^{-2x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}={e}^{-x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96e409f5-e17c-5d02-9a72-f1ba50380ab3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427174}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427177 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b716eb-d4fa-5066-8213-092129b7937e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427145} {:answer/id #uuid "496af30b-c5c5-5345-8110-0135b11a161c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427147} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6e2856-d996-5ac5-aa97-b729e3339896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427153} {:answer/id #uuid "dd26cb8c-0ca5-5aee-9310-8a7a0375c7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427160} {:answer/id #uuid "f59fd045-73ad-5d29-a771-d22dc5113969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427160} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e78623-cec4-5788-b3df-a921432e23fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5da78f32-e695-5e02-8dc1-34ad1449602a" :question/math-academy-id "q-102804" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{3x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})-6u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-{u}^{′}-3u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{3x}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{3x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{-5x}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e444fcb5-e5b0-58fa-a276-f1b31f0b5d06"}] :activity/title "Reduction of Order" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427144}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120724} :activity/id #uuid "e15d96c3-59f3-5ea3-8c30-d6c68ac605ad" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049322 :topic/id #uuid "ebecad61-07fb-5ecb-9dcd-758d5fa56d82" :topic/math-academy-id 1810} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121054 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the shortest distance between the point $P(3,0,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ that is given by the equation \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\langle 2 , 3 , 1 \\rangle ) \\cdot \\langle 4 , 4 , 2 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, note that $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 4 , 4 , 2 \\rangle$ is a vector that's normal to the plane.\n\n![](images/7b/7b2dfcd87c1f5e4e2c2b1a0b9a7f73899772e33e1020465b2b38d1b2de29a66c.png)\n\nLet's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(2,3,1)$. Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,1⟩-⟨3,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe shortest distance $d$ from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26e494eef47db4dba3ac36dfa96c7e9a9d191321b22bb82874f3e74bbd7116ef.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+12+6|}{\\sqrt{16+16+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|14|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is $\\dfrac{7}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "224c4104-9097-5a92-9c37-5d0fe22171a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3261 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9e02b271-c1ce-518a-b549-4e83a2061ea7" :step/math-academy-id 17940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119839 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119653 :question/id #uuid "38fce106-2ba8-554c-ace3-a5731d15a635" :question/math-academy-id "e-4949"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f53201a-0d75-5f17-b869-009c8e0523c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049653}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516850}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Plane Given in Dot Product Form"} :step/id #uuid "3779ed6b-358c-5101-8bd7-9de56abc2ade" :step/math-academy-id 17941 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119654 :question/id #uuid "74693316-2e5a-5821-87a5-fdc5b8719c82" :question/math-academy-id "e-4950"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a50c5f1-fbda-5c87-afa2-32495c69c18d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443268}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Plane Given in Simplified Dot Product Form"} :step/id #uuid "213ff9e2-fe7a-528b-b535-4cf580c6aca9" :step/math-academy-id 17942 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120727}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119655 :question/id #uuid "1538dde4-ab62-502e-8efe-7f37628ab861" :question/math-academy-id "e-4951"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "489887a0-7013-5fb2-8f16-d18616196b0f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476957}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Plane Given in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "fdb4920a-a24d-544c-bdef-93ed800bfdd9" :step/math-academy-id 17943}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120020}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477001} :activity/id #uuid "607e9414-4918-5745-b10c-99f76f09cb79" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049322 :topic/id #uuid "ebecad61-07fb-5ecb-9dcd-758d5fa56d82" :topic/math-academy-id 1810} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b72bdaf-c17d-5a6f-a26c-13328e45c2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260407} {:answer/id #uuid "63966b3b-7d0f-548b-957a-4189afb5ebfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260734} {:answer/id #uuid "654695db-ae2f-5c0a-8492-ff72aa97f271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260751} {:answer/id #uuid "ae41e977-6f41-536e-a881-63dadca54693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261412} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ec70a3-7571-53d4-a32a-f4786362a4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260734} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0d9b85-acc6-5956-920a-859f666ff369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8fc468d7-8c5a-51cb-a422-a6e8deebdd30" :question/math-academy-id "q-129166" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-2,3,-6)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-x+2y+2z=8.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-2$ and $y=1$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2y+2z & =8 \\\\ -1(-2)+2(1)+2z & =8 \\\\ 2z & =4 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-2,1,2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-2,1,2⟩-⟨-2,3,-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-1,2,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0-4+16|}{\\sqrt{1+4+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|12|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "14537fa3-6b22-5ad7-8a11-c7c69ec06d24"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129b0757-3ac6-5753-b6e3-478a8023eb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260037} {:answer/id #uuid "4de222b6-19ed-5d3c-a87c-f1b937df4849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260536} {:answer/id #uuid "65ac176a-0b06-5588-9781-22d9e0435725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260758} {:answer/id #uuid "816e3b5d-f9fa-5bd9-b29d-3f7e216e3a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261004} {:answer/id #uuid "df122527-7c53-5616-83fd-4d0c7dd7847e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260536} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffc75d90-c023-5fb9-a940-576505f511c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0498861d-e4e1-5581-aee5-353d6a78f4f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-129102" :question/problem "Given that $Q(4,-2,8)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-3,6,2⟩=-8$, find the shortest distance from $P(3,-4,2)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-2,8⟩-⟨3,-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-3,6,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 6+6\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-3+12+12|}{\\sqrt{9+36+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|21|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{7} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "40058c5d-013f-5ce7-9c88-237da66a5575" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8f6350-fb69-5a5b-bb29-e322e7c392a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260261} {:answer/id #uuid "5f24cf3d-cea3-54d1-9eda-cbee7058c868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260695} {:answer/id #uuid "78b2e517-ef5a-50b6-a3c1-b0a7028cba72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260933} {:answer/id #uuid "964b77d2-f08c-5b17-8d73-500caab4ed24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261176} {:answer/id #uuid "baf43eb1-d821-5887-8947-2e31570cd345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260695} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb5524e-d28d-538f-a91e-26dfa96b9bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c91ce04-7e86-5b0f-8e36-5b36844cedf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-129103" :question/problem "Given that $Q(3,-4,5)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-2,4,-4⟩=-42$, find the shortest distance from $P(1,-5,2)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-4,5⟩-⟨1,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-2,4,-4⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-4)|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+4-12|}{\\sqrt{4+16+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-12|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5bfda559-d502-5665-9d67-d803ab540b42" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1b5af0-a05c-505e-b5bf-10afc44fa4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476267} {:answer/id #uuid "3016c831-e8f4-5651-b74c-6377c71dd95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476309} {:answer/id #uuid "4275d3b1-6448-558a-b4a5-a4d62b2ac4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476347} {:answer/id #uuid "cebc6772-d1ae-5b50-bd1a-7a34f67a24f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476663} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5a4b5d-4377-54c9-bfe2-c832ba673c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476700} :answer-field/id #uuid "adece7a5-0744-52d2-a422-6e0bf364c79d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bf386a8a-8501-5e3e-a330-283d65ae0e3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129154" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,3,7)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-2x-3y+6z=16.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-2$ and $y=0$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-3y+6z & =16 \\\\ -2(-2)-3(0)+6z & =16 \\\\ 6z & =12 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-2,0,2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-2,0,2⟩-⟨5,3,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,-3,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-2,-3,6⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-7)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+(-5)\\cdot 6|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(6)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|14+9-30|}{\\sqrt{4+9+36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-7|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c4143c0c-c3e3-5771-b847-04afefbdec44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476980}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c37932-b8d1-516f-abf3-f1748834a7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312239} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3b0406-2681-5fe8-a9d6-e948d2f27a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312486} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7e35c1-be2d-55da-bd15-0ac93254cb95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312817} {:answer/id #uuid "a1272a18-c467-54d3-9f71-ae126c7eade1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313242} {:answer/id #uuid "e1afee6d-ef0d-5bae-8b1d-2c7e13dcdbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{26}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312817} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0d43f73-b51f-5e33-b3f5-c33dee8bb5b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "245878e4-0685-5305-b361-ba9fd660a2d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-34074" :question/problem "Given that $Q(2,4,1)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-6,2,9⟩=5$, find the shortest distance from $P(6,-1,0)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,4,1⟩-⟨6,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-6,2,9⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-4)\\cdot (-6)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 9|}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|24+10+9|}{\\sqrt{36+4+81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|43|}{\\sqrt{121}} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fcc02eae-d041-5167-9e1c-3e49457a629e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476984}}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476200}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494044} :activity/id #uuid "3b334ba4-5fdb-57ae-98dd-9bfdc4e4e648" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049322 :topic/id #uuid "ebecad61-07fb-5ecb-9dcd-758d5fa56d82" :topic/math-academy-id 1810} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10263ca4-b8bb-5a2d-9cb0-a6cb6798e4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345488} {:answer/id #uuid "3fee28ed-fd4e-5e57-aea2-936eead49d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345901} {:answer/id #uuid "41336bd9-815f-549b-befc-b646e89179a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345907} {:answer/id #uuid "b507ac70-f33c-5dda-9141-f543ced2a2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346928} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c2d75f-e4ec-55e7-b876-ff7f73776f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347546} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c6cb45-6adc-5f19-b537-ff2e27ce7534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0953b145-a938-579d-a5dc-6946f5364a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-80122" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-1,11,10)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-2x-4y+4z=16.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=2$ and $y=3$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-4y+4z & =16 \\\\ -2(2)-4(3)+4z & =16 \\\\ 4z & =32 \\\\ z & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(2,3,8)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,8⟩-⟨-1,11,10⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-8,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-2,-4,4⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|3\\cdot (-2)+(-8)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 4|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6+32-8|}{\\sqrt{4+16+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,18\\,|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ef6544a-325c-5bf4-ad8a-26a2a5872b84" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a1bf0d7-f3ab-56da-9f69-e8fe7acdca78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260643} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa2f25e-eac8-573f-a22c-70c7051fc5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260945} {:answer/id #uuid "9e90c74b-9340-5886-925a-6fd3dc9f6d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261250} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e32ad6-74af-5732-bdfa-a17a20a619ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261455} {:answer/id #uuid "fac998bb-1203-5d71-bc3a-98304a447645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260945} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eac6dd3-6114-5470-be8e-c3ff6f56e4f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7213d75b-15a3-5beb-aba1-e3068433b4a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-129120" :question/problem "Given that $Q(5,-1,9)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨3,-6,-2⟩=3$, find the shortest distance from $P(7,-2,8)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,9⟩-⟨7,-2,8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨3,-6,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-2)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6-6-2|}{\\sqrt{9+36+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-14|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8f674b71-819d-50c4-9b12-f63f0a340a25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f6631c-af7b-53a2-83af-ac437e78d90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345363} {:answer/id #uuid "619b3823-d10c-5f60-8462-b61b0c00f5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346199} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2d9bb2-2fd3-5983-804f-4a5cd3d99900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346278} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddb3ea6-0413-5720-b61a-ab23c633ab83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346583} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb2fcdd-6bc9-502b-bf63-760bd1212667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345363} :answer-field/id #uuid "9873e119-5653-5fff-9565-620b8e2698f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32d1647f-476a-5352-bcda-228c154bf2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-80120" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-11,-8,-4)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $14x+8y-8z=-6.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-1$ and $y=-2$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14x+8y-8z & =-6 \\\\ 14(-1)+8(-2)-8z & =-6 \\\\ -8z & =24 \\\\ z & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-1,-2,-3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-1,-2,-3⟩-⟨-11,-8,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10,6,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨14,8,-8⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|10\\cdot 14+6\\cdot 8+1\\cdot (-8)|}{\\sqrt{(14)^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-8)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|140+48-8|}{\\sqrt{196+64+64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,180\\,|}{\\sqrt{324}} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96f5b1a1-014f-5d45-8c0e-94671204d88b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "529cadbf-44d1-50b2-8c20-a9e272e22eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346057} {:answer/id #uuid "5889a9dd-d93f-5d3e-9d82-5347ba316df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346111} {:answer/id #uuid "88fb52ad-2071-5801-963f-3a749de660f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346540} {:answer/id #uuid "967e30a0-e60c-522f-a7eb-07ded0736475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346667} {:answer/id #uuid "f5938001-9365-567e-a131-44fd2b8da5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346111} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d59c58c-d76d-581e-8244-df4e793dba6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d51e9464-bbda-5ef7-95c6-2040c203954f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80115" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-2,11,1)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $r\\cdot ⟨2,-5,4⟩=20.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a point on the plane. If $r=⟨x,y,z⟩,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-5,4⟩ & =20 \\\\ 2x-5y+4z & =20 \\\\ 4z & =20-2x+5y \\\\ z & =5-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $x=2$ and $y=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nz=5-\\frac{1}{2}(2)+\\frac{5}{4}(4)=9.\n$$\n\nSo, the point $Q(2,4,9)$ lies on the plane. \n\nNow, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,4,9⟩-⟨-2,11,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-7,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨2,-5,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(4)\\cdot 2+(-7)\\cdot (-5)+8\\cdot 4|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|8+35+32|}{\\sqrt{4+25+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|75|}{\\sqrt{45}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4599a6c-9c22-5014-bf38-93bab9ca1f70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c91748b-039e-5eb9-a37e-a538a11b547b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259994} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b3c3c-b4f8-526a-9901-0c30d02dc50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260689} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8f1a7d-4a09-5032-a136-33ad7657156e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261398} {:answer/id #uuid "e02b39e4-a270-5bce-b071-29e9f45c2040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261855} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ac539e-67e6-579d-a5b0-ddb5f34a0f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261855} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63e23f4-baa1-51c3-b321-bbcaaaa933a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "358dc254-c65c-55e3-933d-25bd9221af6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129165" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-1,4,-5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-2x+2y+z=6.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=1$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+2y+z & =6 \\\\ -2(1)+2(1)+z & =6 \\\\ z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,1,6)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,1,6⟩-⟨-1,4,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-2,2,1⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2+11\\cdot 1|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4-6+11|}{\\sqrt{4+4+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe57e0c2-67ad-59f1-9759-bf1442fb9137"}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493372}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525091} :activity/id #uuid "efa2f5ef-e6fe-56c3-a086-47568aefef07" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049322 :topic/id #uuid "ebecad61-07fb-5ecb-9dcd-758d5fa56d82" :topic/math-academy-id 1810} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5c914f4-5a8b-548c-a5c2-f94ad127ef12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117660} {:answer/id #uuid "e142cff3-c8cc-592a-ad56-bb1b8e198189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{62}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117661} {:answer/id #uuid "00eddfed-b6f0-56e7-ae4c-5028b970d7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117662} {:answer/id #uuid "2d32c0ff-487f-539b-9acc-2fcc848f4231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117663} {:answer/id #uuid "6601c7bc-b43b-5770-bdcb-2d6468033d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186117662} :answer-field/id #uuid "20e14158-31b8-583a-aedb-9f5f098f4e19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6cd2b067-45df-537d-b860-ed07175f92e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-33464" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,9,-6)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $3x+2y+7z=-9.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=2$ and $y=3$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+7z & =-9 \\\\ 3(2)+2(3)+7z & =-9 \\\\ 7z & =-21 \\\\ z & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(2,3,-3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,-3⟩-⟨5,9,-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-6,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨3,2,7⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-3)\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 7|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{7}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-9-12+21|}{\\sqrt{9+4+49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,0\\,|}{\\sqrt{62}} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $P$ lies on the plane."} :step/id #uuid "4ebaacee-c917-59e0-9efc-2e133291f49e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdb367a-ee31-52bd-8e49-76ea1a5deb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{42}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312716} {:answer/id #uuid "81295061-9129-54aa-abdf-5c3501c97825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313016} {:answer/id #uuid "b83ec77e-cb86-58e7-b5e7-11f319ededff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313407} {:answer/id #uuid "c84ad60d-9ec0-5f78-8c5d-b836c1212756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{42}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313539} {:answer/id #uuid "e03a1af2-0bc1-5341-b0a6-801b7d85e33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{42}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313407} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb3b460-9b49-5397-9192-f15cd9be5b1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b172ee6-d9cd-537d-b8f8-03c5ed9b48c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-34062" :question/problem "Given that $Q(4,0,-3)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,5⟩=-11$, find the shortest distance from $P(-2,1,-1)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,0,-3⟩-⟨-2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,-1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨1,-4,5⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 5|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6+4-10|}{\\sqrt{1+16+25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,0\\,|}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $P$ lies on the plane $Π.$"} :step/id #uuid "8c864f64-ba38-5a69-aef8-6cd249fd9aa2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525146}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98b0f40d-33e9-55be-8f3b-7f8eedd896c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261199} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb12be0-0f55-5f3e-88c4-ed8956e836d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261569} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b6f646-82da-5b62-aee6-709895af763d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261739} {:answer/id #uuid "f57cbc53-9eea-50bd-9941-2fb64d7aac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262021} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d686d0-5802-573c-8388-4963787721a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa62847-d308-543b-baa2-afe755afbc10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "875980ed-8584-5326-b58b-3349b4a511e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-129163" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-2,5,-3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $4x-4y+2z=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=1$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-4y+2z & =2 \\\\ 4(1)-4(1)+2z & =2 \\\\ 2z & =2 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,1,1)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,1,1⟩-⟨-2,5,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨4,-4,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|3\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-4)+4\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|12+16+8|}{\\sqrt{16+16+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|36|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{6} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d1bf76c1-13f5-53ce-b624-03ea4de1d4f3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae5898c-91fd-500e-a18d-9a613793916b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345586} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf469ee-bfa5-54ad-9ee7-3e8686851ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345859} {:answer/id #uuid "547fec09-9ac4-53fe-bc5f-235373563b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346067} {:answer/id #uuid "55089e0f-7f2d-5f0a-af43-9bef0b0757bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346071} {:answer/id #uuid "75641616-9478-59af-a091-d9b5df69b71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346385}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346071} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a0dcf4-c48c-52c6-ad65-ce1c700d5ba5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13132844-a558-54b3-bea9-4e5aeb81c059" :question/math-academy-id "q-80118" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-7,5,2)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $4x-8y+8z=-36.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-1$ and $y=1$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8y+8z & =-36 \\\\ 4(-1)-8(1)+8z & =-36 \\\\ 8z & =-24 \\\\ z & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-1,1,-3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-1,1,-3⟩-⟨-7,5,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,-4,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨4,-8,8⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-8)+(-5)\\cdot 8|}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{8}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|24+32-40|}{\\sqrt{16+64+64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,16\\,|}{\\sqrt{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de00c837-e9c3-5a67-b184-8bf559752fe5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525131}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525146 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea53033-050f-51bc-b1f7-d809e2c747fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345473} {:answer/id #uuid "15e2daf7-c934-50bc-ae67-edd8a7622e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345541} {:answer/id #uuid "4b334642-8d19-5d6c-b2b1-468b896c54c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345997} {:answer/id #uuid "a56010e2-ec46-5521-a5cd-8ec3d456b5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346794} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ca087f-3cf0-544b-a843-4b5a0d5368c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345997} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea9fa5e-7580-5121-9429-ea3965fd0aec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "799391e6-f2b4-56d4-b2e4-fcfb4ba4b2c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-80049" :question/problem "Given that $Q(1,3,4)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,0⟩=2$, find the shortest distance from $P(5,-2,3)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,3,4⟩-⟨5,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-4,2,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-4)\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 0|}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|16+10+0|}{\\sqrt{16+4+0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|26|}{\\sqrt{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f2cfe04d-a967-51d9-b573-04f9a99a2b6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525136}}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524375}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551431} :activity/id #uuid "539d761e-4a66-5a50-b7b6-b417f5df20a3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049322 :topic/id #uuid "ebecad61-07fb-5ecb-9dcd-758d5fa56d82" :topic/math-academy-id 1810} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176d1602-8bf5-5374-a9e6-a11eb56ac6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345558} {:answer/id #uuid "83e40ffd-82c5-58c3-8195-51839d2315b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346497} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8eab09-cf42-5d1f-8fb8-df0aa5204ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346865} {:answer/id #uuid "b94ca2ef-7b7e-5956-9d9f-d401dbbd3af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346961} {:answer/id #uuid "d59bd84a-2cfd-5ffe-8072-125a2f3ba837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347230}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346497} :answer-field/id #uuid "736791d0-361f-54ac-a1c5-a82daca0b4af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "972f430d-f05f-5657-9e37-d9ee4828f532" :question/math-academy-id "q-80123" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-4,-1,5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-4x-2y+2z=16.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-1$ and $y=2$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-2y+2z & =16 \\\\ -4(-1)-2(2)+2z & =16 \\\\ 2z & =16 \\\\ z & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-1,2,8)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-1,2,8⟩-⟨-4,-1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,3,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-4,-2,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|3\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-12-6+6|}{\\sqrt{16+4+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,-12\\,|}{\\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22a4c316-56c0-5848-adb3-ad83851869be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551465}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197bde4a-499b-599d-85d4-fececf5a4c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312157} {:answer/id #uuid "39dd64e5-e64f-52c7-a252-928ef189f344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312446} {:answer/id #uuid "852ee814-04e9-5368-9076-4b3fcebb9c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{26}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313046} {:answer/id #uuid "cac3a008-d07d-5f6d-820b-f9604469598b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313558} {:answer/id #uuid "f017a355-98b1-5291-af6b-152a8f2467c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312446} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d1fda63-5573-5ee3-9a63-c10505b09ac7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2558d4d-1eea-5e1f-b2f5-44cc93092823" :question/math-academy-id "q-34075" :question/problem "Given that $Q(1,3,5)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-4,1,-3⟩=-16$, find the shortest distance from $P(-3,7,3)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,3,5⟩-⟨-3,7,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-4,1,-3⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4\\cdot (-4)+(-4)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3)|}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-16-4-6|}{\\sqrt{16+1+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-26|}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "95635de5-b7cc-5b55-b41d-de111b9362f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59951a54-81a6-5810-af8e-c4fdad49ec94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365885} {:answer/id #uuid "a90ec159-7db0-5edb-89ff-ff9b5943472c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365886} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5bdcd7-37da-5f22-aead-d95e3ecf08f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{61}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365887} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c49b79-c479-5e32-8877-9be777b5b8ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365888} {:answer/id #uuid "1dacb56b-d8ef-5624-9327-cee1233132b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365885} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ca0aa0-a220-5bdc-b0d2-65b3a735f48d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a0536ad-4890-5a1a-ae11-f816e5c8b4de" :question/math-academy-id "q-34073" :question/problem "Given that $Q(0,5,2)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-6,2,3⟩=16$, find the shortest distance from $P(1,-4,5)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨0,5,2⟩-⟨1,-4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,9,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-6,2,3⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot (-6)+9\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 3|}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6+18-9|}{\\sqrt{36+4+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|15|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2aa3b79-c6a6-59a0-ad57-3c97a3628133" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "396b6e30-20dc-505a-a011-b9a1c89898fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260387} {:answer/id #uuid "a3012ac3-d9a2-5ff4-a1ed-0eb949759b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261297} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b0aa32-5925-507f-9087-7ad6312dd4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{30}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261593} {:answer/id #uuid "cddd5a3e-4436-57c5-884b-22c96aa491db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261693} {:answer/id #uuid "ddeb2cc5-b0c3-5e7a-a0e2-5c557307f90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261297} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5c4d58e-7136-568a-902e-1affdf5888ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "04f29aba-0bc5-533b-80be-91fd747ce6a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-129158" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-1,3,-4)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $6x+2y+3z=9.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=0$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+2y+3z & =9 \\\\ 6(1)+2(0)+3z & =9 \\\\ 3z & =3 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,0,1)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,0,1⟩-⟨-1,3,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨6,2,3⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 6+(-3)\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 3|}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|12-6+15|}{\\sqrt{36+4+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|21|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{7} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7e7fe84-9da4-5fb8-b4d8-3230d5d24a65"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9e69ab-b00a-5f83-86a8-b4708dff104a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260136} {:answer/id #uuid "253b574b-5f0b-579f-93ef-65a2ff360ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260198} {:answer/id #uuid "4a93d3f3-1c72-5baf-8f80-e4e2091cc5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260516} {:answer/id #uuid "ba31d7f4-c8e0-58a1-8d13-abb4b39225bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261509} {:answer/id #uuid "e34fe396-22d3-55fc-ad47-2c6af61b5bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261509} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae129e17-84fe-5fe6-a754-7cfbb90c8057" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70fdcc6d-7515-5a08-8576-979fc8c2e25e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129119" :question/problem "Given that $Q(1,-3,7)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨6,3,-2⟩=-17$, find the shortest distance from $P(2,-6,2)$ to $Π.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-3,7⟩-⟨2,-6,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨6,3,-2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 6+3\\cdot 3+5\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6+9-10|}{\\sqrt{36+9+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-7|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f12b15ef-1bbe-5947-b51e-ebdfbe5010c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551454}}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550347}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120656} :activity/id #uuid "b4e56add-13ad-5fc4-ab0a-d5396c88286c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051083 :topic/id #uuid "7fcb6c0b-c77e-5abe-8b04-125d08ffe36e" :topic/math-academy-id 1780} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121029 :tutorial/content "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f8c6994-f448-5679-8d2e-5591b86c2a97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3178 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d1731663-b6e6-5bf1-a62a-a1b6ab66b554" :step/math-academy-id 17360 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119605 :question/id #uuid "f7324b66-d8f7-5bdb-9786-4160808f2a7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4768"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048947}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478404}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"} :step/id #uuid "02b00fc5-d232-5394-bd52-5c011f1db096" :step/math-academy-id 17361 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119606 :question/id #uuid "58955d86-8a94-5174-83fa-072314037298" :question/math-academy-id "e-4769"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504696}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "214a0b2c-29b3-58b2-a7c5-0300d4430cab" :step/math-academy-id 17362 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119607 :question/id #uuid "50685ac4-9980-5841-a669-783f6086a9dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4770"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504625}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "dffaaec1-80eb-5582-bd94-3dde9c7b5f0c" :step/math-academy-id 17364}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of 3x3 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120002}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485619} :activity/id #uuid "7ca713a1-c187-592a-a21b-5554571eefb5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051083 :topic/id #uuid "7fcb6c0b-c77e-5abe-8b04-125d08ffe36e" :topic/math-academy-id 1780} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f515a37-0532-5379-b6c5-60cae121cd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379388} {:answer/id #uuid "0f26d137-daef-5ba0-b02f-8ef0beb9e1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379389} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8445fa-f6b7-5338-b384-7962fded25ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379390} {:answer/id #uuid "88a45256-4352-5161-b66d-e81037757768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379391} {:answer/id #uuid "1acc907a-8f9c-5345-b778-5b939debd5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379391} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebcf7832-5de6-5a02-b8c1-346ae0bc9869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a83c5862-ce81-57be-a501-0eb1330527bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-32681" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53e85912-331b-5699-ad74-eadda1b72d11" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0432df21-0cbf-5310-b63d-d6d35b252ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299948} {:answer/id #uuid "0e624142-c08f-57f0-ac81-3c8acbe9f224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300009} {:answer/id #uuid "934f2563-3041-51a5-be22-78f3bc46ae92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300701} {:answer/id #uuid "ac425112-4037-539d-b950-dc90094cf5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300823} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c403d6-3218-5d7c-bb7b-d08ac835de9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300009} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0579342-0bba-5c5a-a576-6b032097c4cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "912ad134-e874-55d5-a192-6e414d5ad134" :question/math-academy-id "q-32533" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.5){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.5,{u}_{33}=1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=0.5.$"} :step/id #uuid "58a9139f-0f38-5e66-8912-d90da7af7fcd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5da53a-52b7-5054-b17e-10efb6d5d1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484941} {:answer/id #uuid "6feaaa27-6016-50e2-ab67-806898239c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485057} {:answer/id #uuid "991fd0bd-bc76-5b7e-872e-9c22a18a7550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485147} {:answer/id #uuid "c59dd4d5-dd58-59a9-80c3-46d088dbc409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485242} {:answer/id #uuid "f927a745-51e1-571d-b0fb-d5b522daaeab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485057} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fc75bcb-654b-5ec8-b6e8-acbbd32d2b3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "485b91ef-f664-5968-befd-b6e37e67e294" :question/math-academy-id "q-32628" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cb85e9d0-18a4-5459-8547-1265563039d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cb4e22-4527-5cdf-a246-1015081dac6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241398} {:answer/id #uuid "45ff70ac-0687-5b4f-8a03-e3801fde7dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241811} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e14ccb-77c2-5ea4-bf3e-93ea6b71314d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242893} {:answer/id #uuid "c7019515-4ac5-5e22-b2ba-750fda287b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242938} {:answer/id #uuid "f01bc462-8fff-5df6-8acb-52fca6d667fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243312} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d43a551-8858-5c8b-93c4-57c4ba597de0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "54845a6f-21d5-55bc-a564-7e1e09132e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106855" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=8.$"} :step/id #uuid "d3dfa4b7-7eba-5707-9e00-359c7727e120" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006f8bad-4488-5247-b030-e69c65d69aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241189} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0029bc-5dd1-5f3b-a321-0734ae89305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242011} {:answer/id #uuid "c5338b5d-9d5a-53f8-8757-89e53bce6a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242918} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3972a3-507c-51c5-a785-eebe4b438e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242969} {:answer/id #uuid "de80bb7f-5c83-554e-90e1-3ead07f6bbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242011} :answer-field/id #uuid "27b176de-565d-5931-ae28-b4d1ef631a50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b30a7824-ca9d-5133-93d0-3fa15525aa26" :question/math-academy-id "q-106858" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,$ ${u}_{33}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "f63ce5de-dd03-522f-a64d-c2d8324c6a7a"}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of 3x3 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484808}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504728} :activity/id #uuid "9cf